{"claims":[{"claim_text":"Support for stating minimum requirements for possible alternatives to single-payer health insurance while doing everything possible to ensure single payer gets a fair hearing.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"Any plan must include a strong public-provision component that can compete with private insurance options.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"All employers and individuals must be eligible to choose the public plan, possibly during an annual open-enrollment period.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[2]},{"claim_text":"The plan must be government run, operated by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services or by a similar agency.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[3]},{"claim_text":"The public plan must have the ability to negotiate drug prices.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[4]},{"claim_text":"The public plan must be allowed to negotiate reimbursement rates, possibly pegged to Medicare rates.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[5]},{"claim_text":"The public plan must, like Medicare, allow participants to choose their own doctors.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[6]},{"claim_text":"Without the provisions of allowing all employers and individuals to choose the public plan, government operation by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services or a similar agency, the ability to negotiate drug prices, negotiated reimbursement rates, and participant choice of doctors, whatever comes out of Congress will not be real reform.", "claim_types":["evaluation"],"evidence_ids":[6]},{"claim_text":"Health care is a human right and must be available to all without economic barriers.", "claim_types":["evaluation"],"evidence_ids":[7]}],"unresolved_references":[]}
